────────────────────────────────────────── Final Decision Document ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberation Decision Document
**Feature Request:** Project managers should be able to see the full history of engagement with a country 
before starting a new mission.

**Termination Reason:** `CONVERGENCE_ALL_ISSUES_RESOLVED_OR_ESCALATED (CRITIC_SIGNALED_CONVERGENCE)` | **Final
Disagreement Score:** `0.0` | **Confidence (Proposer / Critic):** `0.78` / `0.45`

## Executive Summary
Version 1 adds a read-only Engagement History tab to the Country record and an informational summary on the 
Create Mission screen. Both show only the Engagements the user can already see, filtered per record by 
Mission/Project access. The tab lists existing Engagement records with 200-character summary truncation, basic
filters, Contact status labels, and one access-log entry for each open. The main trade-off was exposure and 
data staleness against zero new data entry and no new schema. The Critic's five challenges (C1-C5) were each 
answered with cheap query, display, and logging changes and ended ACCEPTED in round 2. No scope is pending and
no issue was escalated, so no human decision is currently required for this release.

## 1. Agreed Scope
### Included in Scope (v1 MVP)
- [S1] Read-only 'Engagement History' tab on the existing Country record, listing existing Engagement records 
for that country in reverse-chronological order (date, type, linked Contact, linked Project/Mission, summary 
text shown truncated to the first 200 characters with an expand-on-click control), with server-side 
pagination. Opening the tab writes one entry (user, country, timestamp) to the existing application 
access/audit log; no new table and no review screen.
- [S2] Access to the history tab inherits the existing Country/Mission permission model, applied per record: 
every Engagement row is filtered by the same Engagement-level Mission/Project access check used in existing 
Engagement views. An Engagement is never shown merely because the user can see its Country or a linked 
Contact. No new roles, flags, or per-country settings.
- [S3] Pre-mission entry point: on the 'Create Mission' screen, once a country is selected, show a link/inline
summary (engagement count, date of most recent logged engagement, count of currently active missions) that 
opens the S1 history view. All figures are computed after the same per-record permission filter as S1 and 
labelled 'visible to you'. If the user lacks access to the selected country, show only the message 'No access 
to this country's history - request via your Regional Coordinator' with no counts and no link. Purely 
informational; it does not block or gate mission creation.
- [S4] Basic filters on the history view using existing fields only: date range, engagement type, and linked 
Project/Mission. No saved views and no free-text search beyond the existing summary field search, if already 
available.
- [S5] Every Engagement shown displays its linked Contact's name and role as currently stored, with a link to 
the Contact record and a fixed note that contact details are current, not as of the engagement date. Contacts 
marked inactive or departed are visibly labelled, and a blank or unknown status is labelled 'status unknown'. 
The Contact's existing system 'record last updated' date is shown. Existing fields only; no new data entry.

### Pending Human Decision (relies on an escalated issue)
- None.

### Explicitly Excluded from Scope
- Any new data entry by Regional Coordinators: no new required fields, tags, or 'mission relevance' flags on 
Engagements
- New entities or tables (no separate 'History' or 'Briefing' object); the view is a query over existing 
Country, Contact, Mission, Project, and Engagement records
- AI-generated summaries, scoring, or 'relationship health' indicators
- Export to PDF/Word, printable briefing packs, or email digests
- Backfill, cleanup, or migration of historical engagement data; v1 shows what exists as it exists
- Mandatory acknowledgment or approval step before a mission can be started ('must review history' gate)
- Per-record sensitivity or classification tagging and redaction beyond the existing access model
- Cross-country or regional rollup views (for example, all engagements across a region)
- Edit or annotation of engagement history from the history view; it is strictly read-only
- Notifications or alerts when new engagements are logged for a country
- Defining 'full' history to include sources outside the CRM (email, calendar, document stores)
- Point-in-time (as-of-engagement-date) snapshots of Contact name and role, and any coordinator-maintained 
'last verified' field
- A dashboard, report, or review queue for the access log; the log is write-only in v1 and is consulted only 
during incident investigation
- Retention cutoffs or redaction of old engagement summaries (a human policy decision)

### Success Criteria
- For 100% of the ~100 member countries, the Engagement History tab loads the first page (up to 50 records) in
under 3 seconds at the 95th percentile, including countries with 1,000 or more engagement records. (measures 
S1)
- Permission test: in acceptance testing with at least 4 user profiles, including a PM with access to only one
Mission in a multi-mission country, 0 Engagement records are displayed that the user cannot view elsewhere in 
the CRM, and 0 Engagements from the other missions or projects appear for the single-mission PM, including 
Engagements reachable only via a Contact link. (measures S2, S1)
- Summary consistency test: for every test profile, the S3 engagement count and most-recent date equal the 
total and top row of the S1 tab for that same user, and the active-mission count includes only missions the 
user can access (0 discrepancies). (measures S3, S2)
- No-access test: for a user without access to a selected country, the Create Mission screen shows the 
no-access message and 0 counts, dates, or mission numbers in 100% of at least 5 test cases. (measures S3)
- Within 60 days of release, at least 60% of new missions created have a history-view open for the same 
country by the creating user within the prior 24 hours, as measured from the existing access log. (measures 
S3, S1)
- Summary text longer than 200 characters is truncated with an expand control in 100% of tested records, and 
100% of history-tab opens in testing produce exactly one access-log entry with user, country, and timestamp. 
(measures S1)
- Zero new required fields are added to Engagement, Contact, Mission, Project, or Country forms, verified by 
comparing schema and form definitions before and after release. (measures S1, S3, S4, S5)
- Filtering by date range, type, or linked Project/Mission returns correct results in 100% of a test set of at
least 20 filter combinations, and each filtered view returns within 3 seconds at p95. (measures S4)
- 100% of Engagements linked to a Contact whose existing status is inactive, departed, or blank display a 
visible label ('inactive', 'departed', or 'status unknown') in the history view, verified against a test set 
of at least 20 such records, and every Contact shows its record-last-updated date. (measures S5)
- Pre-release check: before launch, a one-time query documents which Countries the PM role can currently 
access and the result is recorded; no access rule is widened for this release. (measures S3, S2)

## 2. Surfaced Assumptions & Deliberation Outcomes
- **[A1] 'Full history of engagement' means all Engagement records already stored in the CRM for that country 
(directly or via its contacts, projects, and missions), with no retention cutoff in v1. It does not include 
external systems.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C2 challenged the no-retention-cutoff history and exposed summary text. It ended ACCEPTED after 
the Proposer added 200-character truncation with expand-on-click and access logging, and left retention 
cutoffs out of scope.
- **[A2] Project managers already have an existing CRM role and can reach Country records and the Create 
Mission screen. The feature is a new read view, not a new user type.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: 
`REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C5 challenged whether PMs can reach every Country. It ended ACCEPTED after the Proposer defined a
no-access message with no counts and added a one-time pre-release check of PM Country access, with no access 
widened.
- **[A3] The existing Country/Mission access model is the authority on who may see which Engagement records, 
and any institutional sensitivity or classification of engagement content is already reflected in that model. 
Deciding whether any engagement types need stricter handling is a policy decision for humans, not part of this
MVP.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C1 challenged whether the existing permission model protects an aggregate history view. It ended 
ACCEPTED after S2 was revised to filter per record by Mission/Project access and the tests added a 
single-mission PM.
- **[A4] 'Before starting a new mission' means surfacing history at the point of mission creation (an easy, 
visible route), not enforcing that PMs read it.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C4 challenged the Create Mission summary figures leaking hidden records or implying a dormant 
country. It ended ACCEPTED after the figures were computed after the permission filter, labelled 'visible to 
you', and covered by a consistency test.
- **[A5] Existing Engagement and Contact data is good enough for a first release: Engagements carry a date, 
type, and link to a Country; Contacts carry a status field. Gaps and inconsistencies in older data are 
accepted and shown as-is.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C3 challenged stale or blank Contact data. It ended ACCEPTED after the Proposer added a 'status 
unknown' label, the Contact's last-updated date, and a fixed note that details are current. Point-in-time 
snapshots and a 'last verified' field were left out of scope.

## 3. Open Questions Requiring Human / Policy Sign-Off
- None. No issue was escalated to humans.

